<mujoco model="2wheel_arm_slider">
  <!--
    Sibling of 2wheel_arm/rotate: same body + fixed-direction arm layout, but
    the arm-side joint is a prismatic slide instead of a hinge. The arm itself
    is rigidly welded to the body (no joint on r_arm/l_arm); instead the
    wheel carriage slides back and forth along the arm's own straight line,
    from the arm's base (near the body, qpos=0) out to the wheel's fully
    extended position at the arm's tip (qpos=0.2408319, the arm's length).
    Unlike the rotate variant's shoulder hinge, a physical linear rail has a
    real mechanical start/end, so this joint is deliberately range-limited
    rather than left unbounded.

    r_arm_motor/l_arm_motor are <position> actuators with gear=4.1522738 (the
    reciprocal of the joint's range, 1/0.2408319 -- MuJoCo's position actuator
    holds ctrl == gear*qpos at equilibrium, so the gear needs to be the
    inverse of the physical range for ctrl to land in [0, 1]), so ctrl in
    [0, 1] maps linearly onto qpos in [0, 0.2408319] -- ctrl=0 is the arm
    base, ctrl=1 is the tip, exactly the "0-1 along the arm" control the
    slide is meant to expose. An earlier version used an <intvelocity>
    actuator (matching the wheel motors' style), but that has its own
    internal integrator state acting as a second spring in series with the
    joint's damping, which combined with a high kp made the carriage bounce
    like a spring instead of settling. A plain <position> actuator has no
    such internal state -- ctrl is the target length directly -- and kv adds
    explicit velocity feedback (a real PD controller).

    kp=100/kv=3.48 were tuned empirically, not guessed -- MuJoCo's position
    actuator force is gear*(kp*(ctrl - gear*qpos) - kv*gear*qvel), so the
    *effective* joint-space stiffness/damping are kp*gear^2 and kv*gear^2
    (gear=4.15 -> gear^2=17.24). kp=100 alone (effective ~1724 N/m) is not
    the problem -- a swept test with a critically-damped kv (~47, effective)
    held it rock solid up to kp=1600 with zero oscillation. What actually
    caused the spring-like bounce was kv too large relative to kp/inertia
    (effective kv ~200-300, i.e. actuator kv ~12-17): with the simulation's
    explicit-Euler integrator, a damping force computed from the *start*
    of the timestep's velocity can overshoot and flip its own sign once kv
    gets that large, which reintroduces oscillation instead of removing it
    -- confirmed by sweeping kv at fixed kp=1724 and seeing the same
    instability reappear only above ~kv=200. kv=3.48 (effective ~60, a bit
    above critical for margin) stays clear of that band. Verified end to end
    via build_model with gravity and the voxel terrain: resting at ctrl=0
    sags ~0.9mm off the base (negligible), and commanding ctrl=1 reaches the
    tip (qpos=0.241) with zero measurable oscillation.

    timeconst="0.5" adds a first-order low-pass filter (dyntype=filterexact)
    between ctrl and the position target: instead of the target jumping to
    the new ctrl instantly, it exponentially approaches it with a 0.5s time
    constant (~63% of the way after 0.5s, ~95% after 1.5s, ~99% after 3s).
    kp/kv are unchanged and still hold firmly once settled -- this only
    slows down the *transition* when ctrl changes, giving the slide a
    damped, gradual feel instead of snapping straight to the commanded
    position.

    Wheel size/mesh is shared with 2wheel_rover/normal (./mesh/wheel.stl,
    copied verbatim); white wheels, same as 2wheel_arm/rotate.

    Self-contained (mesh lives under this robot's own directory) so it can be
    loaded standalone via MjSpec and attached onto sim/xml/world.xml by
    sim/world.py -- the root body has no pos offset, sim/world.py places it
    via a spawn frame.

    r_motor/l_motor drive wheel spin (the project convention: sim/simulate.py
    only ever commands these two, and never touches r_arm_motor/l_arm_motor,
    so with ctrl defaulting to 0 the carriage rests at the arm base).
  -->
  <compiler angle="radian"/>

  <asset>
    <mesh name="wheel" file="mesh/wheel.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001" inertia="exact" refquat="1 0 0 0"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="2wheel_arm">
      <joint name="body_free_joint" type="free" damping="1"/>

      <body name="main_body">
        <!-- same cylinder as 2wheel_rover's main_body_geom (radius 0.05), but 1.5x the width: half-length 0.065*1.5=0.0975 -->
        <geom name="main_body_geom" group="2" type="cylinder" fromto="0.0975 0 0 -0.0975 0 0" size="0.05" mass="0.5"/>
      </body>

      <site name="marker" pos="0 -0.0005 0.001" size="0.05" type="sphere" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>
      <site name="IMU" pos="0 0 0" size="0.01" rgba="1 0 1 1"/>
      <site name="GYRO" pos="0 0 0" size="0.01" rgba="0 1 1 1"/>
      <site name="VELOCITY" pos="0 0 0" size="0.01" rgba="0 1 1 1"/>

      <!--
        The "wheel" mesh asset's own file-origin is not at its geometric center:
        MuJoCo's mesh compiler bakes a +0.04 shift into every geom's x position
        at compile time regardless of what pos is written here (confirmed
        empirically -- 2wheel_rover/normal compensates for this with asymmetric
        wheel mount offsets, 0.07 vs -0.15). Here we cancel it directly on the
        geom (pos="-0.04 0 0") so the mesh's geometric center lands exactly on
        the wheel joint's rotation axis (which passes through the wheel body's
        own origin) -- any nonzero y/z here would put the disc off-axis and
        make it spin eccentrically (wobble), which is not needed/wanted.
      -->
      <body name="r_arm" pos="0.11 0 0">
        <geom name="r_arm_geom" group="2" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0.16 -0.18" size="0.012" mass="0.1"/>
        <body name="r_wheel" pos="0 0 0">
          <joint name="r_arm_joint" type="slide" axis="0 0.16 -0.18" limited="true" range="0 0.2408319" damping="0.3" frictionloss="0.01"/>
          <joint name="r_wheel_joint" type="hinge" axis="-1 0 0" damping="0.3" frictionloss="0.01"/>
          <geom name="r_wheel_geom" group="2" type="mesh" mesh="wheel" mass="0.25" pos="-0.04 0 0" rgba="1 1 1 1" friction="1 0.1 0.1"/>
        </body>
      </body>

      <body name="l_arm" pos="-0.11 0 0">
        <geom name="l_arm_geom" group="2" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0.16 -0.18" size="0.012" mass="0.1"/>
        <body name="l_wheel" pos="0 0 0">
          <joint name="l_arm_joint" type="slide" axis="0 0.16 -0.18" limited="true" range="0 0.2408319" damping="0.3" frictionloss="0.01"/>
          <joint name="l_wheel_joint" type="hinge" axis="-1 0 0" damping="0.3" frictionloss="0.01"/>
          <geom name="l_wheel_geom" group="2" type="mesh" mesh="wheel" mass="0.25" pos="-0.04 0 0" rgba="1 1 1 1" friction="1 0.1 0.1"/>
        </body>
      </body>

      <!-- trailing skid: still only left/right ground contacts (wheels are colinear along x), so pitch stabilization is needed just like 2wheel_rover -->
      <geom name="stabilizer" group="2" type="box" size="0.01 0.07 0.005" pos="0 -0.09 -0.06" euler="0.7853981634 0 0" mass="0.2"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <intvelocity name="r_motor" joint="r_wheel_joint" gear="1" kp="1" actrange="-inf inf" ctrllimited="true" ctrlrange="-5.2359877 5.2359877" forcelimited="true" forcerange="-2.94 2.94"/>
    <intvelocity name="l_motor" joint="l_wheel_joint" gear="1" kp="1" actrange="-inf inf" ctrllimited="true" ctrlrange="-5.2359877 5.2359877" forcelimited="true" forcerange="-2.94 2.94"/>
    <position name="r_arm_motor" joint="r_arm_joint" gear="4.1522738" kp="100" kv="3.48" timeconst="0.5" ctrllimited="true" ctrlrange="0 1" forcelimited="true" forcerange="-30 30"/>
    <position name="l_arm_motor" joint="l_arm_joint" gear="4.1522738" kp="100" kv="3.48" timeconst="0.5" ctrllimited="true" ctrlrange="0 1" forcelimited="true" forcerange="-30 30"/>
  </actuator>

  <sensor>
    <accelerometer name="accelerometer" site="IMU"/>
    <gyro name="gyro" site="GYRO"/>
    <velocimeter name="velocimeter" site="VELOCITY"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
